the Middle East is a region of alliances  divided between two big military powers  the United States and Iran now the  United States has military bases across  the Middle East  and its allies include Israel Jordan  Saudi Arabia and many of the Gulf states  Iran for its part is allied with present  Assad's regime in Syria Hezbollah in  Lebanon the Houthi forces in Yemen and  elements of the Iraqi administration  Iraq also has strategic partnerships  with China and Russia and some limited  ties with Qatar and Oman four decades  ago Iraq invaded Iran half a million  people on both sides were killed and a  truce was eventually agreed in 1988 Iran  retook most of its lost territory the  war  triggered Iran's more expansive foreign  policy that we see today and its pursuit  of nuclear weapons and it built  reputations of some senior commanders  including Qasem Soleimani  in the 1990s and early 2000s the u.s.  fought two wars against Iraq and finally  captured Saddam Hussein in 2003  ever since US forces have been based  there on iran's border much to the  dislike of tehran in recent weeks iran  has conducted big joint naval exercises  with russia and china for the first time  in the gulf of oman and iran has  regularly and repeatedly used its proxy  forces in Yemen Syria and Iraq as well  as Lebanon to attack US interests and  their allies after the storming of the  US Embassy in Baghdad just a few days  ago President Trump had clearly had  enough for 20 years  major general Soleimani was the  architect of Iran's foreign policy in  the region Iran will not replace him and  his influence easily
 

